    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d388">

                <pb facs="0763" n="751" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg751"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Draft of Additional Instructions for Field Teams
                        Entering Manchuria</hi><anchor n="4" xml:id="d388-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-09">April 9, 1946</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>We, General Chen Cheng, Representative of the National Government of China, and
                    General Chou En-Lai, Representative of the Communist Party, have recommended to
                    Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek and Chairman Mao Tze-tung and have been authorized
                    by them to announce that the following provisions are applicable to the
                    situation in Manchuria: <list>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Further movement of Communist-led troops in Manchuria will
                            cease.</item>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Communist-led troops presently occupying positions on or near
                            railroads referred to in the Sino-Soviet treaty of 14 August, 1945, will
                            withdraw from these positions so that the nearest element is at least
                            one day’s march to either flank in order to permit free passage and use
                            of those railroads by National Government forces.</item>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Communist-led troops will evacuate all towns and cities astride <pb
                                facs="0764" n="752" xml:id="pg752"/> of or along railroads referred
                            to in the Sino-Soviet Treaty so that the nearest element is at least one
                            day’s march therefrom.</item>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>.</label>
                        <item>National troops will not pursue or harass withdrawing Communist troops
                            or Communist troops which, complying with terms <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >a</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >c</hi> above, are in the vicinity of railroads and towns and cities
                            referred to within those terms.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>The aforesaid measures are offered as an interim solution. Problems and questions
                    not covered will continue under discussion of the Committee of Three.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d388-f1" n="4" type="source" xml:id="d388-f1-content">
                <p>Prepared by Lt. Gen. Alvan C. Gillem, Jr., as a fourth draft on this subject for
                    conference with Gen. Chou En-lai.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
